From b19cf687aa472b43f01603a2a8f3ab22a882e6ce Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt Turner <mattst88@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 21:57:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] ia64: Properly handle weak undefined TLS symbols

This is the ia64 counterpart of commit ffea43122dc ("alpha: Properly
handle local weak undefined symbols").

A reference to a weak undefined TLS symbol that isn't dynamic resolves
to 0.  elfNN_ia64_relocate_section still treated that 0 as an address
in the TLS segment.  When there is no TLS segment the link fails with

  missing TLS section for relocation LTOFF_TPREL22 against `x'

which is what happens to ld-elf/pr34165.c when it is linked as a
non-PIE dynamic executable.  When there is one, the .got entries for
@ltoff(@tprel()) and @ltoff(@dtprel()) are set to 0 minus the segment
base, and that subtraction can overflow TPREL14, TPREL22, DTPREL14 and
DTPREL22, which is reported with the same message.

Unlike alpha, don't require the symbol to be hidden.  GCC for ia64
doesn't emit .hidden for an undefined weak symbol, so it reaches the
linker with default visibility.

bfd/
	PR ld/34165
	* elfnn-ia64.c (elfNN_ia64_relocate_section): Leave the value of
	an undefined weak TLS symbol as 0 for TPREL and DTPREL relocs,
	and don't require elf_hash_table (info)->tls_sec.  Likewise
	for LTOFF_TPREL22 and LTOFF_DTPREL22 when the symbol isn't
	dynamic.
ld/
	PR ld/34165
	* testsuite/ld-ia64/tls-undef.s: New.
	* testsuite/ld-ia64/tls-undef1.d: New.
	* testsuite/ld-ia64/tls-undef2.d: New.
	* testsuite/ld-ia64/tls-weak-undef.s: New.
	* testsuite/ld-ia64/tls-weak-undef1.d: New.
	* testsuite/ld-ia64/tls-weak-undef2.d: New.
	* testsuite/ld-ia64/tls-weak-undef3.d: New.
	* testsuite/ld-ia64/tls-weak-undef4.d: New.
	* testsuite/ld-ia64/tls-weak-undef5.d: New.
	* testsuite/ld-ia64/tls-weak-undef6.d: New.
